<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-characteristic-activity">

	<title>Bonus module: Characteristic of a ring <m>R</m></title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Multiplication (by Integers) is Repeated Addition</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>
		
<p>
  Throughout the course, students have sometimes written things like <q><m>2[5]_{6}</m></q>, where we are multiplying the integer <m>2</m> by the
  element <m>[5]_{6}</m> in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m>.
  In this section, we will talk about how integer multiplication (and
  exponentiation) work, and how this leads to the idea of the characteristic of
  a ring.
</p>

<definition>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>x\in R</m>.
      For every <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m> we define
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>nx = \underbrace{x+x +\cdots + x}_{n \text{ terms }}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{n} = \underbrace{x\cdot x \cdots x}_{n \text{ factors}}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
  What should we do about <m>0</m> and negative integers???
</p>
<p>
Let's make some conjectures! For all <m>x\in R</m> and <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m>,
</p>
</introduction>

<task>
      <p>
        <m>0x = </m> <fillin characters="8" />
      </p>
</task>

<task>
      <p>
        <m>(-n)x = </m> <fillin characters="8" />
      </p>
</task>

<task>
      <p>
        <m>x^{0} = </m> <fillin characters="8" />
      </p>
</task>

<task>
      <p>
        <m>x^{-n}= </m> <fillin characters="8" />
      </p>
</task>
</exercise>

<p>
  Compute the following based on the definitions and your conjectures:
</p>

<exercise>
<p>
  In <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m>:
</p>
<sidebyside>
<p>
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>3[5]</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>0[4]</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>(-4)[2]</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>[3]^{4}</m></task>
</p>
<p>  
  <task workspace="0.75cm"><m>[2]^{0}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.75cm"><m>[3]^{-5}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.75cm"><m>[5]^{-3}</m></task>
</p>
</sidebyside>
</exercise>

</page>

<page>
<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  In <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>
</p>

<sidebyside>
<p>
	<task workspace="0.25cm">
  <m>0 \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp;\pi \\ -\pi &amp; 3\end{bmatrix}</m>
	</task>

	<task workspace="0.25cm">
  <m>3 \begin{bmatrix}e^{2}&amp;e^{3} \\ e^{3}&amp;e^{4}\end{bmatrix}</m>
	</task>

	<task workspace="0.25cm">
  <m>-4 \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp;\pi \\ -\pi &amp; 3\end{bmatrix}</m>
	</task>

	<task workspace="0.25cm">
  <m>\begin{bmatrix}\pi &amp;0 \\ 0 &amp; \pi\end{bmatrix}^{4}</m>
	</task>
</p>

<p>
  <task workspace="0.5cm">
  <m>\begin{bmatrix}e^{2}&amp;e^{3} \\ e^{3}&amp;e^{4}\end{bmatrix}^{0}</m>
	</task>
	
	<task workspace="0.5cm">
  <m>\begin{bmatrix}e^{2}&amp;e^{3} \\ e^{3}&amp;e^{4}\end{bmatrix}^{-3}</m>
	</task>
	
	<task workspace="0.5cm">
  <m>\begin{bmatrix}\pi &amp;0 \\ 0 &amp; \pi\end{bmatrix}^{-5}</m>
  	</task>
</p>
</sidebyside>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
  In <m>\mathcal{P}_{3}</m>.
  Recall the elements are all subsets of <m>\{1,2,3\}</m> and
  <m>A+B = A\Delta B</m> and <m>AB = A\cap B</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside>
<p>
  <task workspace="0.25cm"><m>0\{ 1,2\}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.25cm"><m>3\{2\}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.25cm"><m>(-4)\{2,3\}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.25cm"><m>\{3\}^{4}</m></task>
</p>
<p>
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>\{1,3\}^{0}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>\{1,2,3\}^{-3}</m></task>
  
  <task workspace="0.5cm"><m>\{1\}^{-5}</m></task>
</p>
</sidebyside>
</exercise>

<definition>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For all <m>x\in R</m> and <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m>,
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>0x = \underline{\hspace{.5in}}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>(-n)x = \underline{\hspace{.5in}}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{0} = \underline{\hspace{.5in}}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{-n}= \underline{\hspace{.5in}}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
</page>

<page>

<paragraphs>
<title>
  The Characteristic of a Ring
</title>

<p>
  The characteristic of a ring allows us to make some interesting observations,
  like <q>the Freshman's dream.</q>
</p>

<p>
  Before we define characteristic, let's consider some questions.
</p>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  For which positive integers <m>k</m> does <m>k[3] = [0]</m> in
  <m>\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  For which positive integers <m>k</m> does <m>k[4] = [0]</m> in
  <m>\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  For which positive integers <m>k</m> does <m>k[5] = [0]</m> in
  <m>\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  Are there positive integers <m>k</m> such that <m>k[x] = [0]</m> in
  <m>\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m> for <em>all</em> <m>[x]\in\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  Are there positive integers <m>k</m> such that <m>kx = 0</m> for all
  <m>x\in \mathbb{Z}</m>?
</p>
</exercise>

<definition>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
      The <term>characteristic</term> of <m>R</m>, denoted <m>char(R)</m> is
      the smallest positive integer <m>k</m> such that
      <md>
        kx = 0_{R} \text{ for all $x\in R$}.
      </md>
      If no such integer exists, then <m>R</m> is said to be
      <term>characteristic zero</term>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>
  A classmate of yours claims that <m>char(\mathcal{P}_{n}) = 2</m> and offers
  the following argument:
</p>

<p>
  Let <m>S\in\mathcal{P}_{n}</m>.
  Then
  <md>
    2S = S + S = S\Delta S = \emptyset = 0_{\mathcal{P}_n}.
  </md>
  Thus, <m>char(\mathcal{P}_{n}) = 2</m>.
</p>

<p>
  Is the classmate's claim correct?
  Is their argument correct?
</p>
</exercise>

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For every integer <m>n\ge 2</m>, <m>char(\mathbb{Z}_{n}) = n</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    Let <m>n\ge 2</m>.
    Note that <m>n</m> is a positive integer, so it is a possible characteristic
    of a ring.
    Note that for all <m>[x]\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>,
    <md>
      n[x] = [nx] = [n][x] = [0][x] = [0].
    </md>
    Let <m>k</m> be any positive integer with <m>k&lt;n</m>.
    It follows that <m>n\nmid k</m> and so
    <md>
      k[1] = [k] \neq [0].
    </md>
    Therefore <m>char(\mathbb{Z}_{n}) = n</m>.
  </p>
</proof>


<paragraphs>
<title>
  Fun facts
</title>

<p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        In general, it is not true that <m>(x+y)^{2} = x^{2}+y^{2}</m>.
        We'd say a high school freshman is <q>dreaming</q> if they think that's
        true.
        It is however true, for <m>x,y \in \mathbb{Z}_{2}</m>, and for any ring
        of characteristic <m>p</m> where <m>p</m> is a prime,
        <md>
          (x+y)^{p} = x^{p} + y^{p}.
        </md>
        You can actually use the <q>Freshman's Dream</q> to test if a large number is
        prime!
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        The characteristic of a field is always <m>0</m> or a <m>p</m> for a
        prime <m>p</m>.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are rings and there exists a ring homomorphism
        <m>\phi: R \to S</m>, then the characteristic of <m>S</m> divides the
        characteristic of <m>R</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</p>

    </paragraphs>
    </page>
 
 </worksheet>

